{"name":"Prickly Pear","hex":"#a89942","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Prickly Pear","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An island of the British Virgin Islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Prickly_Pear"}],"entries":[{"title":"Opuntia","blocks":[{"text":"Opuntia, commonly called the prickly pear cactus, is a genus of flowering plants in the cactus family Cactaceae, many known for their flavorful fruit and showy flowers. Between 8,000 and 9,000 years ago, the Opuntia prickly pear cactus was domesticated in central Mexico. Cacti are native to the Americas, and are well adapted to arid climates; however, they are still vulnerable to alterations in precipitation and temperature driven by climate change. The plant has been introduced to Australia, southern Europe, the Middle East, and parts of Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Opuntia_littoralis_var_vaseyi_4.jpg/330px-Opuntia_littoralis_var_vaseyi_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opuntia"},{"title":"Opuntia ficus-indica","blocks":[{"text":"Opuntia ficus-indica, the Indian fig opuntia, fig opuntia, or prickly pear, is a species of cactus that has long been a domesticated crop plant grown in agricultural economies throughout arid and semiarid parts of the world. O. ficus-indica is the most widespread and most commercially important cactus. It is grown primarily as a fruit crop, and also for the vegetable nopales and other uses. Cacti are good crops for dry areas because they efficiently convert water into biomass. O. ficus-indica is the most widespread of the long-domesticated cactuses. Opuntia species hybridize easily, but the wild origin of O. ficus-indica is likely to have been in central Mexico, where its closest genetic relatives are found."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Dikenli_%C4%B0ncir_%28Opuntia_ficus-indica%29_Gaziantep_Turkey.IMG_1104.jpg/330px-Dikenli_%C4%B0ncir_%28Opuntia_ficus-indica%29_Gaziantep_Turkey.IMG_1104.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opuntia_ficus-indica"},{"title":"Prickly Pear (British Virgin Islands)","blocks":[{"text":"Prickly Pear is an uninhabited island of the British Virgin Islands in the Caribbean. Even though the island doesn't have permanent residents, it has a quiet beach bar called SandBox Bar and Grill and recreational water sports facility on it. It is located on the north side of North Sound, opposite Virgin Gorda."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Prickly_Pear_Island_BVI.svg/330px-Prickly_Pear_Island_BVI.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prickly_Pear_(British_Virgin_Islands)"},{"title":"Pear","blocks":[{"text":"Pears are a genus of trees, Pyrus, in the family Rosaceae, that bear an edible apple-like fruit of the same name. Several species of pears are cultivated for their fruit and juices, while others are grown as ornamental flowering trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Pears.jpg/330px-Pears.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pear"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Prickly_Pear_Closeup.jpg/500px-Prickly_Pear_Closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Prickly_Pear_Closeup.jpg/500px-Prickly_Pear_Closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical morphology","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrickly_Pear_Closeup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Prickly_Pear_2.JPG/250px-Prickl